course: dictionary
lesson: 12904
title: Words that wear away slowly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Waves are eroding the cliffs, so a little more rock falls into the sea every year.» Eroding means slowly wearing away. The clue is «a little more every year»: it happens bit by bit, not all at once.
explain: Rivers, wind and ice can all erode land. «The river is eroding the valley» means the water is carrying away tiny pieces of soil and rock over a long time. To check your meaning, swap in «wearing away» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Eroding is not only about rocks. «Rising prices are eroding my savings» means the money you have is slowly becoming worth less. «Eroding confidence» means someone is slowly feeling less sure of themselves. Look for the feeling of something getting weaker or smaller little by little.
fact: Eroding is the -ing form of erode, so it can describe something happening right now: «The cliff is eroding.»
fact: The other forms of erode are erodes, eroded and erode, all linked to the same meaning.
fact: «Eroding» means slowly wearing away rock, soil or land by wind, water or ice.
fact: «Eroding» can also mean slowly making something weaker or smaller, such as trust or savings.
fact: When you see eroding in a text, ask: is something getting smaller or weaker bit by bit?
exercise: «The sea is eroding the cliff a little more each winter.» What does eroding mean? | Freezing solid in one night | Growing taller and stronger | Painting with bright colours | *Slowly wearing away
exercise: «Rising prices are eroding my savings.» What is happening to the savings? | Being counted and doubled | Being sent to the bank by post | Getting bigger in one big jump | *Getting smaller or weaker bit by bit
exercise: In «The river has been eroding the valley for thousands of years», which words show it happens over a long time? | eroding the | *for thousands of years | the valley | the river
test: «Wind and rain are eroding the rocks into strange shapes.» What are the wind and rain doing? | Lifting the rocks into the sky | Breaking the rocks in one blow | *Slowly wearing the rocks away | Painting the rocks red
test: «Her trust in him is eroding.» What does eroding trust mean? | *Trust is getting weaker little by little | Trust is being sold at the shop | Trust is as hard as a rock | Trust is growing fast and strong
exam: In «The waves are eroding the cliffs», what is the best meaning of eroding? | Building up slowly | Covered in snow | *Wearing away slowly | Shining brightly
